# flux — repository rules

A Jira alternative. pnpm 9 workspace, TypeScript everywhere, `strict` plus
`noUncheckedIndexedAccess` and `exactOptionalPropertyTypes`. Read `AGENTS.md` for
the full picture; this file is the subset you cannot violate.

## 1. Frozen paths — CI fails the PR, not the file

Do not edit anything under these prefixes. The check is
`node scripts/check-frozen-paths.mjs origin/<base>` and it runs on every PR.

| Path | Why |
| --- | --- |
| `db/migrations/`, `db/bootstrap/` | a migration edited after it has been applied anywhere is permanently divergent; role privileges are the tenant-isolation boundary |
| `packages/contracts/` | the shared contract every other layer is built against |
| `packages/mocks/` | every fixture is parsed by its schema at construction — that parse is the UI's only guarantee it renders shapes the API will really return |
| `packages/db-tests/` | a test relaxed to unblock a feature removes the evidence for a security claim |
| `scripts/`, `.github/` | these are what verify the invariants, and what enforces them |
| `eslint.config.mjs`, `tsconfig.base.json`, `.prettierrc.json`, root `package.json`, `pnpm-workspace.yaml`, `docker-compose.yml` | each is a place where one line turns a check into a no-op, with a green tick either way |
| `docs/adr/`, `docs/specs/`, `AGENTS.md`, `CLAUDE.md` | a decision is superseded by a new document, never rewritten; code that edits its own spec cannot be reviewed |

`docs/change-requests/` is the one carve-out, and it is the pressure valve: **if a
contract, a lint rule or a spec is genuinely wrong, copy
`docs/change-requests/TEMPLATE.md` and write it up.** Do not widen a schema so a
component compiles, and do not disable a rule inline. A contract quietly widened
mid-feature is indistinguishable from one that was never designed.

`pnpm-lock.yaml` is not frozen — adding a dependency to your own package
regenerates it. But ask before adding one.

## 2. Contract-first

Import types from `@flux/contracts`. Never hand-write an interface that mirrors
one, and never cast to make a shape fit — the cast is where the bug lands two
layers away (`docs/change-requests/006-board-card-priority-type.md` is the whole
story: one `z.string()` that should have been an enum blanked a board).

Ids are branded and created with `newId<Brand>()`. `crypto.randomUUID()` is a lint
error: ids are UUIDv7 so they sort by creation time.

## 3. The gate, before you hand anything back

```bash
pnpm install
pnpm --filter @flux/contracts build   # everything else consumes its .d.ts
pnpm format:check && pnpm lint && pnpm typecheck && pnpm test
```

Contracts must be built first or typecheck fails on a clean tree. Paste the real
output — the test counts included — into the PR description. "Tests pass" without
a count is not evidence.

`pnpm format` is the formatter *and* the Tailwind class sorter. Run it; never
hand-sort class names.

## 4. Git

- Work on the branch you were given. Never commit to `main`.
- Do not push, open a PR, or change any repository setting unless asked.
- Commit messages: imperative subject, a body explaining *why* when it is not
  obvious, ending with the co-author trailer the tool you are running under uses.

## 5. Quality bar

`docs/product-quality-bar.md` is binding and worth reading once in full. The short
version: a feature that technically works but feels slow, confusing, fragile or
inconsistent is **unfinished**. Every surface is checked against the happy path,
empty, loading, slow network, error, permission failure, large data, refresh,
keyboard, screen reader and narrow viewport. Never show a raw backend error, never
say "Something went wrong" when the cause is known, and a control that silently
does nothing is worse than one that says why it cannot.

Fix what you find. Do not report it and move on.
